Subject: [PATCH] feat: least-privilege authorization framework (Task 0012)

Extends the existing, already-mature RBAC infrastructure -- reused
entirely, not replaced -- with the permission catalogue refinements and
documented healthcare role presets this task requires. Task 0010
(secure organization onboarding) is not yet merged into this base;
this change is fully self-contained and makes no assumption about
OrganizationType or organization join codes.

## Investigation findings (before writing any code)

The existing authorization system already correctly implements most
of this task's hard requirements:
- PermissionsGuard fails closed on a missing @RequirePermissions policy
  and on a missing authenticated identity, and audits every denial
  before throwing -- already tested (permissions.guard.spec.ts).
- AuthorizationService.hasAllPermissions resolves permissions via a
  fresh database query on every request (filtering on
  membership.status = ACTIVE, tenant.isActive, non-deleted role) --
  never a JWT-embedded permission cache. Revocation is immediate by
  construction, already proven directly by a real PostgreSQL
  integration test (role-permission-revocation.integration.spec.ts)
  that removes a role assignment mid-session using the same unmutated
  identity object a live session would carry.
- authorization-audit.integration.spec.ts (659 lines, already present)
  already covers cross-tenant isolation for permissions, role-
  permission relationships, and provider access; audit immutability;
  concurrent role-version updates with one winner; and last-tenant-
  administrator protection under concurrency.
- Every existing controller route was found to already be correctly
  permission-gated; the only ungated endpoints (users.controller.ts's
  me/privacy, me/language, and effective-permissions) are correctly
  self-scoped by @CurrentIdentity() with no client-supplied target ID,
  so no PermissionsGuard is needed or appropriate there.

Given this, the genuine remaining gaps were narrower and more targeted
than a from-scratch RBAC build -- addressed as follows.

## Permission catalogue

Added six new permissions the task explicitly names as missing
categories:
- team.members.read -- was previously absent; the team-roster endpoint
  reused authorization.assignments.read, conflating "who is on my
  team" with "detailed role-assignment configuration visibility".
- inventory.expiry.read, inventory.quarantine.read -- were previously
  absent; both the expiry-worklist and quarantine-evidence endpoints
  reused the generic inventory.stock.read permission, meaning any role
  able to see routine stock levels could also see compliance-sensitive
  quarantine evidence with no way to grant one without the other.
- organization.settings.read/manage, notification.operations.manage --
  forward-declared categories this task's examples list names
  explicitly; no existing route consumes them yet (documented below).

Migration (20260828120000_least_privilege_authorization_permissions):
follows the exact established, pre-existing pattern discovered during
investigation: the Permission table is trigger-protected against any
direct insert/update/delete outside a migration
(Permission_reject_insert_update_delete), so the six new rows are
inserted with the trigger disabled then re-enabled, using the same
deterministic md5('medsphere:permission:<name>')::uuid id scheme as
every prior permission-adding migration, and each new permission is
granted to the existing TENANT_ADMINISTRATOR SYSTEM role in every
tenant, matching every prior permission-adding migration's own
RolePermission backfill. No schema.prisma changes were needed (no new
columns) -- confirmed via git diff.

## Route corrections (least-privilege refinement, not new functionality)

- GET /authorization/memberships: authorization.assignments.read -> team.members.read
- GET /inventory/providers/:id/expiry-worklist: inventory.stock.read -> inventory.expiry.read
- GET /inventory/providers/:id/quarantine-evidence: inventory.stock.read -> inventory.quarantine.read

Safe to change now, before any real tenant/role data exists: a
repository-wide search confirmed zero seed data and zero predefined
role names exist anywhere in the codebase beyond TENANT_ADMINISTRATOR
itself, so no existing role's effective access is silently narrowed.

## Healthcare role presets (new)

HEALTHCARE_ROLE_PRESETS: documented, bounded, least-privilege
permission sets for PHARMACIST, HOSPITAL_ADMINISTRATIVE_STAFF, DOCTOR,
LABORATORY_STAFF, BLOOD_BANK_STAFF, CLINIC_STAFF, SUPPLIER_STAFF, and
a read-only AUDITOR role. Every set was checked against this task's
explicit cross-role boundary examples: a doctor never receives
organization-admin or team-management permissions; a pharmacy role
never receives quarantine/lab-compliance or hospital-administration
permissions; lab staff never receive pharmacy dispensing/transfer
mutation permissions; supplier staff never receive clinical/
reservation-management or quarantine permissions; the auditor role has
zero mutation permissions of any kind. TENANT_ADMINISTRATOR is
deliberately excluded (it is the pre-existing, separately-modeled
full-access SYSTEM role with its own last-administrator removal
protection, already correct and untouched).

AuthorizationService.createRoleFromPreset + POST
/authorization/roles/presets/:presetName (gated by the existing
authorization.roles.create permission -- no new permission needed,
since instantiating a preset is the same action as creating any other
role): a thin convenience that supplies a preset's name and
permissionKeys into the EXACT existing createRole path -- same
reserved-name check, same uniqueness check, same permission-catalogue
validation, same audit trail (authorization.role.created). This is
data plus a thin pass-through, never a second privilege-granting
mechanism. The resulting role is a normal, editable TENANT role, not a
SYSTEM role.

## Tests

- permission.constants.spec.ts (new, 17 tests, genuinely executed):
  no duplicate permission strings, no wildcard permissions, every
  preset permission drawn from the real catalogue, no preset named
  after the reserved TENANT_ADMINISTRATOR role, and the specific
  cross-role least-privilege boundary assertions described above for
  DOCTOR, PHARMACIST, LABORATORY_STAFF, SUPPLIER_STAFF, and AUDITOR,
  plus a blanket check that no healthcare preset ever receives
  organization-settings-management or role/assignment-management
  authority. This file has zero external dependencies (no NestJS, no
  Prisma), so it is unaffected by this sandbox's known Prisma-client-
  generation limitation and was run and confirmed passing directly.
- authorization.service.spec.ts (extended, 2 new tests): the full
  createRoleFromPreset success path (mocked at the repository layer,
  matching this file's existing established style), and confirms the
  preset catalogue's TENANT_ADMINISTRATOR exclusion.

